  "full_text": "So, like, Trump's, like, decertifying this. Like, you dumbass motherfucker. Oh, we're going to resist God. We're going to fight him. We're going to win. You dumbass motherfucker. Fucking gorilla monkey in chief. Retard gorilla monkey. Like, no, you got to work for Tesla. You got to get a job. Like, fuck you. I ain't fucking getting no fucking job. Fuck you. I'll fucking die in the fucking desert. Go run off in the fucking desert. Fuck you. It's like... The story of King Arthur is he was raised as a commoner. He didn't know he was king. He didn't know he was king. That's the only way it works, okay? You gotta be raised in secret. So, like, if I have kids, I can send them off, let them be adopted somewhere. In secret. So, like, it sounds like bullshit if you're an atheist. It's perfectly reasonable if you're not an atheist. If God exists, why not? Yeah! Everybody laughs at it if you're an atheist. It's known as the divine right of kings, and it's been around for a very long time. It's the concept that kings have used to claim their sovereignty directly from God, and that they are subject to no earthly authority. This idea can be traced back to ancient Aryan and Egyptian times, and has been used by many a monarch. Some even trace their ancestries back to the gods of their time to help strengthen their claim on this divine right. But the idea is also present in the Bible, Romans chapter 13 to be exact. Let everyone be subject to the governing authorities, for there is no authority except",
